l ledger.\n\nKeep unevaluated nodes in a FIFO queue ordered by creation; children are appended in their deterministic generation order. Before each node evaluation, if no call remains, return the current ledger with `budget-exhausted`; otherwise evaluate the oldest pending node and charge one call. When no node is pending, return infeasible if every terminal node is certified empty. If every nonempty terminal node has a valid lower bound, let $L$ be the least such bound; when $U$ is finite and $U-L\\le\\varepsilon$, return the feasible witness and global epsilon certificate. Retain bounds for closed nodes in this calculation. Otherwise choose an evaluated nonclosed node with least lower bound, breaking ties by creation order. If an integer interval has at least two values, choose the least-index such coordinate and split at $m=\\lfloor(a+b)/2\\rfloor$ into the disjoint children $[a,m]\\cap\\mathbb Z$ and $[m+1,b]\\cap\\mathbb Z$. Otherwise, if a continuous width is positive, split a longest continuous side at its exact midpoint, using least coordinate index ties. Children replace the parent in the coverage frontier and are evaluated before another split. A mixed singleton node that is neither certified empty nor closed by the gap test returns `inconclusive`.\n\nIf a mixed relaxation bound is unavailable, stop the main tree and, if at least one call remains for each assignment, enumerate every $z\\in Z$ in lexicographic order. Substitute $z$ into the objective and constraint DAGs to form $f_z$ and $C_z=\\{x\\in X:g_i(x,z)\\le0\\text{ for all }i\\}$, then invoke [[alg-certified-spatial-branch-and-bound]] on $(X,C_z,f_z)$ with the continuous budget allocated to that assignment. Allocate the remaining calls evenly, with extra calls assigned to the first assignments. If every assignment returns a complete terminal ledger or a certified-infeasible result, let $L$ be the minimum of all lower bounds on nonempty terminal leaves and let $U$ be the minimum of all feasible upper bounds. Return infeasible if every assignment is certified infeasible; return a global epsilon certificate only when $U$ is finite, $L$ is finite, and $U-L\\le\\varepsilon$. If any assignment has an incomplete ledger or oracle failure, or the aggregate gap is larger, return inconclusive. If enumeration does not fit in the remaining budget, return inconclusive. This fallback is finite because $Z$ is finite. The method makes no polynomial-complexity claim.",
