+          "quote": "Inputs are a $C^1$ objective $f:D\\to\\mathbb R$ on an open $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$, $x_0\\in D$, consistent SPD matrices $B_0,H_0$ with $B_0H_0=H_0B_0=I$, a reset scale $\\gamma>0$, constants $0<c_1<c_2<1/2$, finite line-search budgets $E,Z$, a finite iteration budget $K$, a direction mode exact or rounded-Cholesky, and an update mode undamped or Powell.\n\nInitialize $k:=0$, $x_k:=x_0$, $B_k:=B_0$, $H_k:=H_0$, and $g_0:=\\nabla f(x_0)$. If $g_0=0$, return stationary at $x_0$. While $k<K$, if $g_k=0$, return stationary at $x_k$. Use the Euclidean inner product of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] in descent and curvature tests. In exact direction mode set $p_k:=-H_kg_k$. In rounded-Cholesky mode call [[alg-bfgs-hessian-direction-with-rounded-cholesky]] with $(B_k,g_k)$; if the factorization or triangular solve fails, return its failure status with $x_k$. If the computed direction is not descending, reset $B_k:=\\gamma^{-1}I$, $H_k:=\\gamma I$, and set $p_k:=-\\gamma g_k$.\n\nCall [[alg-bracketing-zoom-strong-wolfe-search]] with $(f,x_k,p_k,c_1,c_2,E,Z)$. If it returns a failure status, return that status and $x_k$. Otherwise take its positive strong Wolfe step $\\alpha_k$, set $x_{k+1}:=x_k+\\alpha_kp_k$, and compute $g_{k+1}:=\\nabla f(x_{k+1})$. Define\n$$ s_k:=\\alpha_kp_k,\\qquad y_k:=g_{k+1}-g_k. $$\nIn undamped mode set $\\bar y_k:=y_k$. In Powell mode put $c_k:=s_k^TB_ks_k$ and use the damping rule of [[def-bfgs-curvature-pair-and-update-formulas]] to form $\\bar y_k$. If $s_k^T\\bar y_k\\le0$, skip the matrix update and return curvature-rejected with $x_{k+1}$. Otherwise update both matrices by\n$$ B_{k+1}=B_k-\\frac{B_ks_ks_k^TB_k}{s_k^TB_ks_k} +\\frac{\\bar y_k\\bar y_k^T}{s_k^T\\bar y_k}, \\qquad H_{k+1}=(I-\\rho_ks_k\\bar y_k^T)H_k(I-\\rho_k\\bar y_ks_k^T)+\\rho_ks_ks_k^T, \\quad \\rho_k=(s_k^T\\bar y_k)^{-1}. $$\nIf any required scalar or matrix operation is undefined, discard partial update results and return update-failure with $x_{k+1}$. After a successful update, commit $(x_{k+1},g_{k+1},B_{k+1},H_{k+1})$ as the state for the next iteration. If $g_{k+1}=0$, return stationary at $x_{k+1}$; if $k+1=K$, return iteration-budget-exhausted at $x_{k+1}$. Otherwise set $k:=k+1$ and continue from that committed state. A line-search failure returns its status with $x_k$. If the loop exits because $k=K$ without another return, return iteration-budget-exhausted with $x_k$; this covers $K=0$. The output is the last point and one of stationary, a line-search status, factorization/solve failure, curvature-rejected, update-failure, or iteration-budget-exhausted.\n\nThe mathematical exact direction mode and the BFGS matrix updates are interpreted over $\\mathbb R$. The rounded-Cholesky direction mode specifies a computed factor-and-solve direction only; this item supplies no backward-error or convergence theorem for that mode. Its returned direction is checked for descent before the strong Wolfe search. The line-search procedure can fail within its finite budgets even when an acceptable step exists.",
